theory.intro:
A **position vector function** $\underset{\sim}{r}(t)$ gives a particle's position at time $t$, measured from the origin. Its components are the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t)$, written $\underset{\sim}{r}(t)=x(t)\underset{\sim}{i}+y(t)\underset{\sim}{j}$. Substituting a value of $t$ gives the position at that time.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-0.6,0)--(6.2,0) node[right,font=\footnotesize] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.6)--(0,4.6) node[above,font=\footnotesize] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue,<->,domain=0.4:5.6,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.35*\x*\x-1.9*\x+3.6});
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (P) at (4.4,2.016);
\draw[dashed,gray] (P)--(4.4,0);
\draw[dashed,gray] (P)--(0,2.016);
\draw[->,thick] (O)--(P) node[midway,above left,font=\footnotesize] {$\underset{\sim}{r}(t)$};
\node[below,font=\footnotesize] at (4.4,-0.05) {$x(t)$};
\node[left,font=\footnotesize] at (-0.05,2.016) {$y(t)$};
\fill (P) circle (2.2pt);
\node[above right,font=\footnotesize] at (P) {$P$};
\node[below left,font=\footnotesize] at (O) {$O$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
$x(t)=1+t$ increases by $1$ each second, and $y(t)=4-t$ decreases by $1$ each second. The particle starts at $(1,4)$ and moves in a straight line, going $1$ unit right and $1$ unit down each second.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[->,black] (-0.4,0)--(5.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-1.1)--(0,4.9) node[above] {$y$};
\node[below left] at (0,0) {$O$};
\draw[->,thick,black] (1,4)--(5.4,-0.4);
\fill[black] (1,4) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[black] (2,3) circle (1.5pt);
\node[above right] at (1,4) {$(1,4),\ t=0$};
\node[right] at (2,3) {$(2,3),\ t=1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
